WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
The simplest way to compare Type I and Type II is point in time versus period of time.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.700
Type I asks whether the controls are suitably designed and in place on a particular date.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.440
Type II asks whether the controls are suitably designed and operated effectively over a

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.020
defined period.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.840
Both reports can be valuable,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.220
but they answer different questions. Imagine an access review control.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.400
For Type I, the auditor may evaluate whether the control is defined, assigned,

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.420
and designed appropriately as of the report date.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.600
The team may show the policy, the owner, the process,

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.460
and evidence that the review mechanism exists.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.240
For Type II, the team needs more.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.540
It needs to show that access reviews actually happened during the review period,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.680
that they covered the right users and systems, and that exceptions were handled.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.380
The same distinction appears in change management.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.000
A Type I report may focus on whether the change control process is designed:

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.860
changes require tickets, review,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.960
testing, approval, and deployment records.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.860
A Type II report needs evidence that actual changes during the review period followed that

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:08.960
process.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.260
It may inspect samples of change tickets and verify that approvals, tests,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.240
and deployment records are present.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.220
Monitoring works the same way.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.280
A Type I report may show that monitoring is designed and configured.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.380
A Type II report needs evidence that monitoring operated during the period,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.280
that alerts were reviewed,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.260
and that escalations or incidents were handled according to the process.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.680
Backup controls, vendor reviews, security training, vulnerability remediation,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.340
and incident response all follow the same basic pattern.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.200
This difference affects preparation and workload.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:50.100
Type I preparation often emphasizes scope, system description, control design, ownership,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.360
policy alignment, and point-in-time evidence.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.780
Type II preparation emphasizes recurring control operation, evidence retention,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.560
exception management, remediation tracking, and review-period discipline.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.700
A team that treats Type II like Type I with extra paperwork will struggle.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.320
The report choice should match the assurance need.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.980
Type I can show that the control environment is ready at a point in time.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.080
Type II can show that controls operated over time.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:22.180
Customers, risk teams, and sales processes may value them differently.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.800
The important thing is to describe the report accurately and prepare for the evidence

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.260
standard that matches the

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.440
report type.
